; -*- mode: lisp -*-
; ============================================================================
; PROBLEM -- what to DO. `tape_peel/handle`, as extended BDDL.
;
; AUTHORED -- there is no upstream .bddl for this task.
;
; provenance.tier: reproduction
; source-of-record: robosimstudio/tasks/tape_peel/suite.py (deleted by this port)
; upstream:         AutoPeel (ICRA 2025), via the reference tape_render /
;                   tape_traj implementation.
;
; Compare this against the same task's hand-written Python suite: every number
; that used to sit in `metadata` as an untyped tuple is here as a typed clause,
; and every number that describes the TAPE rather than the task has gone to
; rgbs/tasks/rgbs/assets/peel/tape/, where what a thing is belongs.
;
; WHAT MAKES THIS ONE INTERESTING. It is the first document with a LATCHING
; goal. `once` freezes success and lets reward keep moving, which is not a
; detail: a broken bond never re-forms, so a peel is not something the world can
; undo -- but the quantity behind it goes on changing, and the shipped evaluator
; reports the live number all the way down. A `once` that latched both would
; freeze reward at the instant of success and diverge from every recorded trace
; on the descending edge.
;
; Sections marked [EXTENSION] are ours. The rest is standard BDDL and means what
; it means upstream.
; ============================================================================

(define (problem PEEL_THE_TAPE_BY_ITS_HANDLE)


  (:language pull the tape strip off the box by its handle)

  ; Typed by the PLACED records: `tape_handle` is the tape's declaration with
  ; the factory that lays it along this layout's longer, lower box with its
  ; rigid pull-tab, and the box record is that box welded where the handle
  ; tip lands on the measured grasp point. Where things are is the asset's
  ; own arithmetic (peel/layout.py); a document names the thing.
  (:objects
    tape - tape_handle
  )

  (:fixtures
    ; The box the strip is stuck to. Scenery: take it away and there is nothing
    ; to peel off, which is the line between a fixture and furniture.
    box - peel_box_handle
  )

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------- [EXTENSION]
  ; :staging -- what BDDL cannot say, and what LIBERO supplies through harness
  ; convention instead: which cell, which robot, which cameras, which physics,
  ; how many episodes. Recorded rather than defaulted, because a task whose
  ; scene came from a convention should say which convention. The cell, the
  ; control mode and the episode count are rgbs.suite's; this is the delta.
  (:staging
    ; The LEFT arm peels and no arm supports. The suite's pairing is
    ; right-works/left-holds, and a staging key is overridden as one clause,
    ; so the whole pairing is restated -- including (:arms-solid true): only
    ; one arm peels, but the other stands over the same box while it does,
    ; and an idle arm is exactly the one nothing was checking.
    (:embodiment yam_i2rt (:work-arm left) (:arms-solid true))
    (:physics cloth_vbd@ref)          ; a named arrangement, never a solver config
    ; The suite rig, aimed just above the box top -- where the bond line is
    ; and where the peel front travels. The aim is the layout's arithmetic, so
    ; the aimed rig is a constant of the layout module, named as an artifact.
    (:cameras peel.layout:HANDLE_VIEWS)
    ; One arm does the work; the hosted pairings below include one-armed
    ; robots, which is what this line admits.
    (:arms 1)
    ; The shipped routine peels well inside 900: its phases are 50 + 30 + 60
    ; + 40 + 220 = 400 control steps. A graph driving the same path needs
    ; roughly twice that -- it settles between stages, opens and closes the
    ; gripper against a settle count of its own, and burns steps idling
    ; while a node does non-tool work. Measured: at 900 the lift was still
    ; streaming when the episode truncated, which scores a peel that was
    ; progressing as a total failure.
    (:max-steps 1800)
  )

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------- [EXTENSION]
  ; :hosts -- the pairings that stand a foreign robot in this cell.
  ;
  ; Each row says only "here is how to stand this robot here", never that the
  ; pairing WORKS: that is a claim about the matrix, discovered by running it,
  ; and a file asserting it would go stale with nothing to notice. Both the
  ; first two are confirmed by scripts/matrix.py: they compile and hold their
  ; declared home posture to 0.0000 rad over a settle. No graph and no routine
  ; claim -- "it stands there correctly" is a long way from "it can reach the
  ; tab", and the matrix does not pretend otherwise.
  ;
  ; `yam_i2rt` is not here: it is what these variants are NATIVELY staged on
  ; since the merge, and a row declaring it as a hosted pairing would be
  ; declaring the task's own staging as an alternative to itself.
  ; `yam_bimanual` took its place -- it is the robot these layouts were
  ; calibrated against, and keeping it reachable is what lets the pre-merge
  ; numbers be re-run. `ur5` is six joints and a hand that was welded on
  ; rather than shipped with the arm; the one-armed layout is where a new
  ; robot arrives, since nothing here needs a second hand to hold anything.
  (:hosts
    (:host (:embodiment yam_bimanual (:work-arm left))  (:notes "compiles; no policy"))
    (:host (:embodiment panda        (:work-arm right)) (:notes "compiles; no policy"))
    (:host (:embodiment ur5          (:work-arm right)) (:notes "compiles; no policy"))
  )

  ; Standard :init, using the domain's own state words. Both are CONSTRAINTS to
  ; be satisfied and verified after settling, not a layout -- which is true of
  ; upstream BDDL's init too, and is the part every harness quietly fills in.
  ;
  ; `anchored` is what makes this layout the layout it is: the tab is held at its
  ; as-laid pose until a gripper takes it, which turns a drooping flap into a
  ; package pull-tab an arm can reach. The withdrawn `along_x` stated no such
  ; thing, and that single missing clause was the whole difference between the
  ; two variants' starting worlds -- and the reason its tab was out of reach.
  (:init
    (adhered tape)
    (anchored tape)
  )

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------- [EXTENSION]
  ; The goal, with two things standard BDDL lacks:
  ;
  ;   (once ...)   a temporal modal. The verdict is STICKY: a broken bond never
  ;                re-forms, so a peel is not something the world can undo, and
  ;                a non-sticky reading would let a successful peel un-succeed
  ;                because the tape fell over.
  ;
  ;   (>= ... )    a graded comparison over a measure. Boolean predicates cannot
  ;                express "essentially every bond has let go".
  ;
  ; 0.95, not 1.0: a handful of bonds under the far edge can sit inside their
  ; break threshold indefinitely while the film is plainly off the box, and a
  ; benchmark that waits for them measures patience.
  ;
  ; Stated over `broken-fraction` and NOT over `(<= (bond-fraction tape) 0.05)`,
  ; which is the same sentence and not the same test. The shipped evaluator
  ; compares the broken share against 0.95; the complement compares 1 - s
  ; against 0.05, and in binary floating point those disagree the moment the
  ; fraction lands exactly on the gate. A port copies the comparison, not the
  ; arithmetic identity.
  (:goal
    (once (>= (broken-fraction tape) 0.95))
  )

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------- [EXTENSION]
  ; :reward -- separate from the goal, and it has to be. Reward is not "the share
  ; of goal predicates satisfied" in six of the nine shipped evaluators: this one
  ; is progress toward a gate, clipped. Note it keeps moving while the verdict is
  ; latched -- `once` latches SUCCESS ONLY.
  ;
  ; The gate is the goal's gate, deliberately. Where they differ (film_peel gates
  ; reward at its success fraction while its goal also demands the film survived)
  ; the difference is the point; here there is none, and writing 0.95 twice is
  ; what makes that visible rather than assumed.
  (:reward (progress (broken-fraction tape) (:gate 0.95)))

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------- [EXTENSION]
  ; :observe -- recorded every step, never gating. These are the quantities
  ; nobody has calibrated. Reporting them uncalibrated is how they get
  ; calibrated; thresholding them uncalibrated is how a benchmark ends up
  ; measuring an arbitrary constant.
  ;
  ; `peel-front` is the interesting one. A peel is not "some bonds broke", it is
  ; a FRONT travelling from the tab inwards, so it counts contiguous fully-broken
  ; width-rows starting at the tab -- a strip torn out of the middle scores 0
  ; here and a high broken fraction, which is exactly the distinction it exists
  ; to make. It is reported monotonically, and that is a property of the
  ; instrument rather than of this task: an apparent retreat is noise on a row
  ; that was marginal, not the tape re-adhering.
  ;
  ; `held-ever` is what separates "the grasp failed" from "the peel failed". A
  ; run that reports neither leaves the two indistinguishable.
  (:observe
    (peel-front tape)
    (peel-front-rows tape)
    (clear tape)
    (held tape)
    (held-count tape)
    (held-ever tape)
  )

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------- [EXTENSION]
  ; :artifacts -- a solution is a thing that RUNS, so it stays code and is
  ; referenced by path. The task is data; the policy is not.
  ;
  ; A GRAPH AND NO ROUTINE, since G2. `tape_peel.routine:PeelRoutine` was named
  ; here and solved this layout; the graph solves it strictly better, measured
  ; over seven fresh processes each -- `broken_fraction` 1.000 against 0.956,
  ; `peel_front` 1.000 against 0.933, `clear` 1.000 against 0.825, with no
  ; variance on either side. Two policies for one peel is two things to keep
  ; true, and the one that was kept is the one that plans each leg from the live
  ; pose instead of replaying a plan.
  (:artifacts
    (:tools   "tools:register_tape_peel_tools")
    (:graph gt "graphs/peel_gt")
  )

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------- [EXTENSION]
  ; :policy-params -- inputs to the ARTIFACT, not statements about the world.
  ; Typed and fenced off deliberately: the moment routine parameters mix with
  ; goal parameters, the untyped side channel this refactor exists to delete has
  ; been reinvented under a new name.
  ;
  ; And they are RECORDS, not the source of truth, which is the second half of
  ; the same lesson. The suite's `metadata` carried `peel_arm: left` beside a
  ; binding that already said `work-arm left`, and `grasp_mode: pinch` beside a
  ; layout that already had a rigid handle -- two copies of two facts, each free
  ; to disagree with its original. The policy reads the originals: which arm
  ; works comes from the pairing, and a pinch is what you use on a tab that is
  ; presented rather than drooping -- the graph's `reach` node derives exactly
  ; that from the tab's own rigidity.
  ;
  ; `(routine-solves true)` was here and has moved to :provenance as
  ; `tab-reachable`. It was a verdict about a policy, which is what this
  ; section's fence says it does not carry, and with the routine deleted it would
  ; have been a verdict about nothing. What was true underneath is a fact about
  ; the layout: this tab is somewhere the arm can reach.
  (:policy-params
    (layout handle)
    (peel-arm left)
    (grasp-mode pinch)
  )

  (:provenance
    (:tier reproduction)
    (:source "AutoPeel (ICRA 2025)")
    ; Whether the work arm can put its tool where this layout leaves the tab.
    ; True here, and it is what this variant exists to be: the pull-tab is pinned
    ; rigid at box-top height, every accuracy-critical landmark solves under a
    ; tenth of a millimetre, and the graph takes it 7/7. `along_x` stated the
    ; other half of the pair -- false, with the graph refusing at `reach` 7/7
    ; without moving -- until it was withdrawn. It is a fact about the pairing,
    ; not a verdict on any policy.
    (:tab-reachable true)
    (:note "No published benchmark defines success for a deformable task like
            this, so the goal is designed. The one quantity with a reference to
            calibrate against is the broken-bond fraction, and it was
            calibrated on the withdrawn along_x layout rather than this one:
            133 bonds formed on that flat strip as in the reference, and their
            aggregate hold matched its 2.0 N. No variant reproduces that
            measurement now; what survives it is the material constants it fitted,
            in peel/tape.py. This layout is the manipulation reference -- a
            tab the arm can actually reach, where every accuracy-critical
            landmark solves under a millimetre and the shipped policy peels the
            whole strip. The scripted routine this document used to name peeled
            96% of the bonds here; it was deleted in G2 and the graph exceeds
            it.")
  )
)
